# Server permissions in JSON

Authorization for a resource lives on the server. JSON carries the viewer's capabilities. The SPA reads those fields.

```ts
// BAD — client re-derives policy
const owned = me?.username === article.author.username;

// GOOD — use the payload
article.permissions.update
article.permissions.delete
article.permissions.favorite
profile.permissions.follow
profile.permissions.update
comment.permissions.delete
```

Keep resource state (`favorited`, `following`) separate from permissions (may this viewer call the action).

Do not bring back `ViewerRole`-style client role models for Edit vs Follow. That pattern was transitional.

Session and routing stay separate (`RequireAuth`, nav "New Post"). Those are authentication, not resource policy.

Extend `@conduit/contracts` when adding permission fields. Do not invent ad-hoc shapes only in the UI.
